Transfer (@transfer: 0, is #chapter, #instinct, #part-of @first-steps, #done) { r_00ECACY50NWQQ3S0YH37V8KMK6

A Transfer is a well structured process for Contributing to other's Needs and having yours met. It might be a way of exchanging physical resources, buying services, exchanging information, proposing the assignment of tasks... It has a few steps: a proposal, an agreement and an action. It is designed to work best with Record's quantities between different Lince Cells.

I start with information, I say what I can Contribute to or Need, another party confirms that it wants to be part of this Transfer, make it real. We then agree on the specifics and do our part. A Transfer can start out only with the Contribution part, used as a brainstorming mechanism, a way to generate consensus: if we model a Need and all involved agree on one specific Contribution, the following actions and automation are an agreed upon plan. If I post a Need (make a Record public as a Need), anyone that wants to Contribute will give a different solution, suggesting to break it down into other Needs and helping with some of them.

We want to be able to automate the Transfers with built-in mechanisms Karma. To not have to think of a frequent purchase, to commit to it, to show first our Needs and Contributions to a certain Organ/Cells, then if they are not interacted with expand the visibility of them to other parties.

I can create a Transfer for a Record as either a Need or a Contribution, might depend on how you see it, use it: let's say I want to play the Guitar live in front of an audience, that can be a Need to perform, or a Contribution as a service, paid or not. Your framing of it as either in the Need part or in the Contribution part will tell people in advance what is your goal. If nobody believes they Need to hear you play they wouldn't hire you, but if you say that you want to be heard playing they wouldn't mind letting you play at an event of theirs, contributing to your Need.

We might only make a Transfer when we reach a certain state: if Record less than 7 then we buy Apples until we have 14 with a certain specific Lince Cell (known farmer). If we know that our 'Apple' Record's 'quantity' property diminishes by one every day, and we have 8, the day after tomorrow we will have 6, hitting the state to activate a Transfer proposal to buy more Apples. The farmer that wants to buy a new tool to produce more will see that they can't make a Transfer with a tool maker to buy that tool because they dont have the money yet. But because they see only the predictability that the transfer will happen after two days they can plan their business better, knowing that the chain of Transfers will happen 2 days from now.

That can extend to several different Transfers, many people agreeing to many things happening so they all happen. You might need 7 previous Transfers to meet Needs of your customers so they can Contribute to other Needs and buy your Apples. Knowing this you can take part in Contributing to those previous Needs so the flow of Contributions can continue flowing, unclogging the Transfers. You may call it Investing In Your Customers, or Just Helping.

"The feature of Simulation is key for this. We might have a Karma to activate a Transfer proposal of donating all extra apples if we hit 20. So if we have 30 we activate that Transfer proposal and wait for someone to collect it. Until then, we see two numbers, the real number" (30) and the number that exists in our Lince for our own use (20), the extra 10 will be donated so they are like free Contribution Apples, if we eat one, now there are 9 Contributions we can make, because our donations are on the surplus. Or we make a rule that the eating of the Apple will remove a number from our non donation apples, and the donation stays the same with the 10 Apples.

- [ ] Coordination/Collaboration
	- [ ] Several parties in same Transfer
	- [ ] Observers
- [ ] Resources
	- [ ] I can setup an amount of resources flowing out of my Organ and going into another Organ, which is weird to write. That means a one way Transfer, functioning more like a donation.
	- [ ] I can compose a two way Transfer, functioning like a Trade.
	- [ ] I can say how much should go to the other party's resources, how much I am willing to contribute with some Record, and have the amount that is publicly being Contributed by me be different from the one i contabilize internally in my Lince: saying I will pay you 10 might make me internally diminish my 'Money' Record by 5 instead. I still will pay 10, but I control how my resources fluctuate with Transfers internally.
	- [ ] Surplus can be projected. (Surplus = quantity you have beyond what is already committed to hard reservations — the "safe to give away" figure. Formula: `record.quantity - reserved_quantity`. Distinct from `available_quantity` in that surplus could also account for confirmed incoming deliveries not yet settled.)
- [ ] Visibility
	- [ ] A party can see only the Transfer fields allowed for it.
	- [ ] A party can see a Transfer item title without seeing the source Record head.
	- [ ] A party can see a Transfer item description without seeing the source Record body.
	- [ ] Visibility can hide source Record identity.
	- [ ] Visibility can hide other parties.
	- [ ] Visibility can hide locations and quantities.
- [ ] Agreement
	- [ ] Agreement state can also be derived for a parent Transfer.
	- [ ] Dependency agreement mode is wired to interaction dependency satisfaction. (Agreement is complete when the depended-on Transfer itself is agreed/settled, not when local parties sign. Requires querying `transfer_interaction` rows with `depends_on` kind and checking the referenced Transfer state.)

# Trust

When interacting with other parties, it is important to know how much you can trust them. If the transfers are public and hard to fake and i can prove this party is the same of those transfers I might filter better my interactions by going to certain Actors. Your public actions start building a portfolio for Trust.
} r_00ECACY50NWQQ3S0YH37V8KMK6

Interface (@interface: 1, is #chapter, #instinct, #part-of @first-steps, #done) { r_99NWECF19VKV94HQQ3G3ASMC4M

# The Zen on Interfaces.

- [ ] Choices can be done quickly. Full control is always available.
	- [ ] Customization
- [ ] Built-in UI is minimal, not taking screenspace from users and flexible, fitting many Needs.
	- [ ] Sands: the widgets/components that live in the Box. Smaller pieces can be composed into groups, with interactions between them and other sands, receiving data from a Protein stream and executing Protein Actions in certain interactions. We should have the fundamental blocks to construct several types of workflows:
		- [ ] Basic elements of buttons, dropdowns, tooltips of an UI library.
		- [ ] Ready made Sands, combined to form Castles: Kanban, Table, Calendar, Graphs, Clock, Games, Transfer and Karma built as the features around it stabilize in backend.
	- [ ] Box
		- [ ] Being able to edit sands, adding more properties associated with them, shown by them, events leaving from them, being read by them, and their consequences. Also editing the UI to use different tokens, or delve into custom CSS styling.
		- [ ] Fundamental areas of data manipulation to emulate the ready made sands: areas to spawn data, areas to pull data in or push away, areas to sort data, areas for immunitiy to those effects. Maintaining everything built as a package from interacting with these Areas of Influence, so buttons can change areas and areas can move buttons around associated with properties that the areas affect.
- [ ] Interfaces should ask for as little attention as possible throughout the user's life. The best interface is Interfaceless.
	- [ ] Interoperability.

# UI Guidelines

The brand is black and white by default, with purple as the supporting color.

Purple Cobalt — CMYK: 66, 71, 0, 36; HEX: `#3730A3`; RGB: 55, 48, 163.
Nocturnal Purple — CMYK: 59, 58, 0, 5; HEX: `#6366F1`; RGB: 99, 102, 241.
Deep Lead — CMYK: 10, 10, 0, 92; HEX: `#121214`; RGB: 18, 18, 20.
Gray — CMYK: 10, 5, 0, 12; HEX: `#A7B4C2`; RGB: 167, 180, 194.
Ice White — CMYK: 2, 1, 0, 1; HEX: `#F8FAFC`; RGB: 248, 250, 252.

Components are flat by default, preffering 2D forces us to convey information only with flat data, not layers and composition, constraining the space forces minimalism. Shadows and hints can be used for subtle differentiation of areas, components and data.

} r_99NWECF19VKV94HQQ3G3ASMC4M

Interfaceless (@interfaceless: 0, #part-of @interface, #v2.0.0) {

We need to model everything, and be able to not interact with Lince to have our needs met. Not necessairly knowing Lince exists, not necessairly continuing the project with the name Lince, quietly disappearing, being absorved by a bigger project, giving it capabilites of Lince. Modeling things in Lince is also a Need. So if i have a Need to model something as big as the observable universe or bigger i should, computers handle scale well, an i128 is enough. Setting a boundary on a part of the universe gives us a sandbox, if we have an Earth globe at our disposal to create these areas we may edit them to escape to fantasy land. I take the place that is my house on the map and put all my Needs and Contributions, relay it as being from my city for privacy. I look at things that are Needed in the areas close to me, or in the other side of the planet, I make transfers with them, of physical resources, information, services... I automate workflows, and I create scenarios on top of visualizations of the real world. I take tools like Gaussian Splatting, photos, videos to see what the world is like in the present, and I overlap it, mesh it together with my assets, the structures I create, using tools like game engines, blender, CAD, Generative World Models to transform the digital representation of the world, taking the real version or the simulated, gamified one and create the Needs and model the Contributions related to it, assign Actors to take care of them. Possibly doing that without interacting with Lince. That's the goal.

For that a system that allows for maximum control of the scenes, is needed, the freedom of Minecraft with real world data and tiny, tiny voxels. That would be the end of the Lince's interface, nothing grater is justified to be built according to the philosophy. It would easily be able to display current web pages, launch other systems and games inside it. You open Lince, it has everything you should Need, and give you freedom to do anything you want in it's digital representation, connected completely with the real world if you want it.

- [ ] Voxel system to go from outer zoom of the observable universe down to atoms (Bevy's Big Space: https://github.com/aevyrie/big_space/tree/bevy-0.19).
- [ ] With the interface that has virtually limitless scale, we focus on centering the experience on the user's interest, Earth's globe as a 3D space with it's real topography, updateable in real time.
- [ ] In any scale, one can take what is there as a point in which the components/widgets/sands/visual tools have at their disposal will be in a size it is good to work with, either locking things to the HUD with pinned sands, or putting them geographically in one region of the space: only in their house when they zoom in the map into their room, or the part of the room in space that is their desk, and inside they will see their widgets. In summary going from 3D to 2D, where you feel more productive with the dimensions you want.
- [ ] Being able to connect the data of parts that are anywhere in the Space.
- [ ] Being able to generate instantly a representation of something you want in any part of the Space, using Fiote (JEPA model?).
- [ ] Analyzing with Fiote what some part of the space we are seeing is, and the possible Needs/Contributions we care about. 
- [ ] View the future simulation of such world based on the possible Automations and Transfers that will happen, as transformations and scenarios of the Space, versions of it, mixed in a same scene, like if 50% transparency, some house that can be built, as if i'm planning a farm in a property and i'm putting down in the simulation where each resource will be, and after setting them, taking the unfinished work, exploding it into each tiny part and Assigning it to contrActors in Lince and doing it myself too.
